"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bc035e05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Sustainable Development, Annual Report 2018-2019 (2019) at pp. 6 and 92-93</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bc035e15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.gov.mb.ca/sd/pubs/annual-reports/annual_report_2018_19.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The structure of the CGPIO is aimed at eliminating a silo approach and duplication and establishes and maintains the new &ldquo;whole-of-government&rdquo; vision required to advance a timely, comprehensive and integrated <ital>Made-in-Manitoba Plan.</ital> The office provided leadership on the development of <ital>The 2018 Climate and Green Plan Act</ital> and supports the work of the Expert Advisory Council established under the Act.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">15</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bc035e25f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Sustainable Development, Annual Report 2018-2019 (2019)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bc05cf05f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.gov.mb.ca/sd/pubs/annual-reports/annual_report_2018_19.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> See <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref"><ref ref.id="I0bc05cf15f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" secref="8:21">&sect;8:21</ref></cite> of this chapter for further discussion of the CGPIO.</para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">As earlier described in <cite finder="manual" type="secondaryxref"><ref ref.id="I0bc05cf25f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" secref="8:18">&sect;8:18</ref></cite> of this chapter, the <ital>Made-in-Manitoba Plan</ital> proposed a &ldquo;Prairie Price&rdquo;&mdash;a low, flat, carbon price of &dollar;25 per tonne for the years 2018-2022.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">16</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bc05cf35f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba Sustainable Development, A Made-in-Manitoba Climate and Green Plan (2017) at p. 15</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bc084005f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://gov.mb.ca/asset_library/en/climatechange/climategreenplandiscussionpaper.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The Federal government, finding that Manitoba&rsquo;s proposal did not align with federal benchmarks, implemented its carbon pricing system in the province commencing April 1, 2019.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">17</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">In 2019 the Federal fuel charge rate reflected a carbon pollution price of $20 per tonne of carbon dioxide equivalent (CO2e), rising by $10 per tonne annually to $50 per tonne in 2022. See <ital>Greenhouse Gas Pollution Pricing Act</ital>, S.C. 2018, c. 12.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Manitoba challenged the federal government and in particular, the federal government&apos;s approach to &ldquo;measuring the stringency of Manitoba&apos;s plan and unequal treatment relative to other provincial plans&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">18</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">See <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bc084015f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Manitoba, &ldquo;Manitoba Confirms No Appeal in Carbon Tax Case&rdquo;, News Release (November 17, 2021)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bc084025f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://news.gov.mb.ca/news/index.html?item&equals;52739&amp;posted&equals;2021-11-17</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The Federal Court decided in favour of the federal government in October 2021 and Manitoba did not pursue an appeal of the decision.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">19</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="case"><ref p1="Manitoba" p2="Canada (Attorney General)" post.rptr="4640" pre.rptr="2021" ref.id="I0bc084035f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" title="Manitoba v. Canada (Attorney General)">Manitoba v. Canada (Attorney General), 2021 CarswellNat 4640, 2021 CarswellNat 4641, 2021 FC 1115, 44 C.E.L.R. (4th) 273, 2021 D.T.C. 5118 (F.C.)</ref></cite>.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> Accordingly, the <ital>Made-in-Manitoba Plan</ital> continues to evolve.</para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">In June 2019, on the advice and recommendations of the Expert Advisory Council<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">20</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">For discussion of the principles taken into account by the Expert Advisory Council to guide its advice and recommendations on the carbon savings account and the GHG emissions reduction goal see <cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bc0ab105f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Report of the Expert Advisory Council to the Minister of Sustainable Development: A Carbon Savings Account for Manitoba, June 2019 (2019) at p. 7</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bc0ab115f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.gov.mb.ca/asset_library/en/eac/eac_carbon_savings_report2019.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> as required under <ital>The 2018 Climate and Green Plan Act</ital>, the Minister of Sustainable Development set Manitoba&apos;s GHG emissions reduction goal for the 2018 to 2022 period &ldquo;at 1 Mt of CO<sub>2</sub>e relative to a forecast of the total greenhouse gas emissions that would have occurred in that period&rdquo; if no additional GHG emissions reduction measures had <?WGXPPrightHandPageStart sp=";;45" nep=";;47" hyphenated="n" folio="8-45" backFolio="8-46" backBlank="n" releaseNumber="4" releaseDate="2022;12;30" ?>been implemented in that period.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">21</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bc0ab125f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Minister of Sustainable Development, Policy: Made-in-Manitoba Climate and Green Plan, Manitoba&apos;s Greenhouse Gas Emissions Reduction Goal for 2018 to 2022 (June 2019)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bc0d2205f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.manitoba.ca/asset_library/en/eac/sd_response.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The 1 Mt reduction goal is a cumulative reduction target amount over the five-year period.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">22</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bc0d2215f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Minister of Sustainable Development, Policy: Made-in-Manitoba Climate and Green Plan, Manitoba&apos;s Greenhouse Gas Emissions Reduction Goal for 2018 to 2022 (June 2019)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bc0d2225f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.manitoba.ca/asset_library/en/eac/sd_response.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text></para><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">With the establishment of the GHG emissions reduction goal, Manitoba announced the establishment of a &ldquo;carbon savings account&rdquo; for 2018 to 2022. Manitoba is the first jurisdiction in North America to establish an economy-wide carbon savings account.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">23</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bc0f9305f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Minister of Sustainable Development, Policy: Made-in-Manitoba Climate and Green Plan, Manitoba&apos;s Greenhouse Gas Emissions Reduction Goal for 2018 to 2022 (June 2019)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bc0f9315f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.manitoba.ca/asset_library/en/eac/sd_response.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022). In October 2021, the Minister of Conservation and Climate requested the Expert Advisory Council to provide advice and recommendations on emission reduction targets for the next carbon savings account 2023-2027.</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> The carbon savings account is &ldquo;Manitoba&apos;s way&rdquo; to &ldquo;drive ongoing emissions reductions&rdquo;.<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">24</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bc0f9325f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Report of the Expert Advisory Council to the Minister of Sustainable Development: A Carbon Savings Account for Manitoba, June 2019 (2019), at p. 6</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bc120405f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.gov.mb.ca/asset_library/en/eac/eac_carbon_savings_report2019.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote> As stated by the Expert Advisory Council,<footnote date.updated="0"><footnote.reference date.updated="0">25</footnote.reference><footnote.body date.updated="0"><para date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0"><cite finder="manual" type="secondary"><ref ref.id="I0bc120415f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5" short="y">Report of the Expert Advisory Council to the Minister of Sustainable Development: A Carbon Savings Account for Manitoba, June 2019 (2019)</ref></cite>, available at <cite finder="manual" type="url"><ref normalpub="webref" ref.id="I0bc120425f4d11ed98ebdf198970e0c5">https://www.gov.mb.ca/asset_library/en/eac/eac_carbon_savings_report2019.pdf</ref></cite> (accessed May 2022).</para.text></para></footnote.body></footnote></para.text><quote.block date.updated="0"><quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Simply put, it is the sum of all emission reductions over a fiveyear period on a cumulative basis. This is tracked against a set cumulative emissions reductions goal for those five years. The emissions reductions are the &ldquo;carbon savings&rdquo;; the tracking against that goal is the &ldquo;account&rdquo;.</para.text></quote.para><quote.para pstart="n" date.updated="0"><para.text date.updated="0">Cumulative emissions reductions is the best method to measure carbon emissions reductions in Manitoba, given the province&apos;s clean electricity grid and the nature of the province&apos;s emissions profile.</para.text></quote.para></quote.block></para></section.body></section><